{"title":"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Partial_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Partial Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_that_involves_a_non-elementary_result\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution that involves a non-elementary result</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Determine which integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/d/49d98ffe996c0f0d3b82b284cba2113439fefea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" /> have the property that there exists an infinite sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/9/5e97a8af68fbc8e357d3ee0eba452022b06c1875.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/2/572b65ebc3f438b176b4ca4a890799f2f72564af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/6/64683f315744c851898c8d65dace1a8aa31b93ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dots$\" width=\"19\" height=\"2\" /> of nonzero integers such that the equality\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/5/bb59afa27abb3e054ab7f2cb301e50ffcca5ad62.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_k + 2a_{2k} + \\dots + na_{nk} = 0\\]\" width=\"208\" height=\"15\" />\nholds for every positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Partial_Solution\">Partial Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Partial Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> equal to any odd prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, the sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/e/c9e84aa66f8cd662b7214bd8e770e61538668bbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\{a_i = \\left(\\frac{1-n}{2}\\right)^{m_p\\left(i\\right)}\\right\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"172\" height=\"53\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/c/3cc2ac1a8dfc1457bae6bafd264c1fe5df094639.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^{m_p\\left(i\\right)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"19\" /> is the greatest power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> that divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, gives a valid sequence. Therefore, the set of possible values for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is at least the set of odd primes.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_that_involves_a_non-elementary_result\">Solution that involves a non-elementary result</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution that involves a non-elementary result\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(Since Bertrand's is well known and provable using elementary techniques, I see nothing wrong with this-tigershark22)\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/b/6cb7ae00764b56ee2adf59e78a1ffde9685b80db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/d/24d820120189679177ec8ae1f1281d5b2914231e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_1| = 2 |a_2| = \\cdots = 2^m |a_{2^m}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"215\" height=\"18\" /> implies that for any positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/9/429c29dcd847a04273761835dca362575a061b0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_1| \\ge 2^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" />, which is impossible.\n</p><p>We proceed to prove that the infinite sequence exists for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/e/6be91d8532fe2a50e227f2fec7c3cecdd8758df3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>First, one notices that if we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/2/b2288a8bf5abc74feb58fb3de2b4f7dea92701f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{xy} = a_x a_y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"12\" /> for any integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, then it is suffice to define all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/5/9a5af51cfec649afb7d8bf2c8b7080b58ba69830.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> prime, and one only needs to verify the equation (*)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/9/e697236810b760244f0113a20e2a4de2b737e8c7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1+2a_2+\\cdots+na_n=0\\]\" width=\"193\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>for the other equations will be automatically true.\n</p><p>To proceed with the construction, I need the following fact: for any positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b54b2efe5c5111c7e6c198528ba6ba294ae0bf8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"12\" />, there exists a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/5/515659377c876c279022c0eea7db73b47ba193d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{2} &lt;p \\le m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"93\" height=\"33\" />.\n</p><p>To prove this, I am going to use <b>Bertrand's Postulate</b> (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertrand&#39;s_postulate\">[1]</a>) without proof. The Theorem states that, for any integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/65262bf247c4a3c6114a8223d249dc9702619d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />, there exists a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/6/a064c848c3b9dd40358eff90a30d33e3a87711f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&lt;p\\le 2n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"122\" height=\"17\" />. In other words, for any positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b54b2efe5c5111c7e6c198528ba6ba294ae0bf8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"12\" />, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/6/bd6c2b6035a39aaeac72c692b7b878b2a060cc03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=2n$\" width=\"59\" height=\"12\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/65262bf247c4a3c6114a8223d249dc9702619d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />, then there exists a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/2/892e76623fe1518eb125eb58971642cfb08ba669.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{2} &lt; p &lt;  m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"93\" height=\"33\" />, and if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/4/9948a1e26389c397083b44d1217afbdaa0586122.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=2n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"90\" height=\"12\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/65262bf247c4a3c6114a8223d249dc9702619d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />, then there exists a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/9/d498d02046027057c459280c02bc886837fae341.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m+1}{2} &lt;p\\le m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"124\" height=\"37\" />, both of which guarantees that for any integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b54b2efe5c5111c7e6c198528ba6ba294ae0bf8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"12\" />, there exists a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/5/515659377c876c279022c0eea7db73b47ba193d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{2} &lt;p \\le m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"93\" height=\"33\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\nGo back to the problem. Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/e/6be91d8532fe2a50e227f2fec7c3cecdd8758df3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />. Let the largest two primes not larger than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" />, and that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/e/3ae0a6979bcab7de318aa3f0612f89147475fdc7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge P &gt; Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"87\" height=\"16\" />. By the fact stated above, one can conclude that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/0/45034b7bda95071bd7fe58622ae0a2d5172de44c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2P &gt; n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" />, and that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/c/bacd3db11f7a288aa4178fdd3f6ccedbb870d0b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4Q = 2(2Q) \\ge 2P &gt; n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"176\" height=\"18\" />. Let's construct <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" />:\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/0/2b02a491e6fc8db3a577b344b1a53b7a8e77e0f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" />. There will be three cases: (i) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/7/4d742f8a8918e0bce2e1e9b459cc0d66a4f79d5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q&gt;\\frac{n}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"51\" height=\"33\" />,  (ii) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/f/abff13e5b21385269cf240090b46bbbb78ef6363.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{2} \\ge Q &gt; \\frac{n}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"34\" />, and (iii) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/b/61bbfae05e9c95bad9d8d664846b522332e0394e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{3} \\ge Q &gt; \\frac{n}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"90\" height=\"33\" />.\n</p><p>Case (i): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/0/6b0c18799e67ca001c81324d2e523f6416598e36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2Q&gt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/e/dfe0da219e49d7d770aad4046d6a16ccb30802fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> for all prime numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e813a6f3f49c506b3f83aed6b27b353e66c5d895.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&lt;Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/1/9f13c3435f98273ac95e512eb9aa55de88f781d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{xy}=a_xa_y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"12\" />, then (*) becomes:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/e/6fe1a9c22e01d6dabc15292ac7ee2a0be4d0f0a0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Pa_P + Qa_Q = C_1\\]\" width=\"134\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Case (ii): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/0/ae06b704b36f8827b7557fe3617c40121daf8893.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2Q\\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/1/f91dec5b710ef36d9cd664bee9217889b79096e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3Q &gt; n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" />. In this case, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/4/d34172a21a18b744f98c75396d9f9910ba4abe60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_2=-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/e/dfe0da219e49d7d770aad4046d6a16ccb30802fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> for all prime numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/e/a2ea362b3408d1be81e9e6efd9eafd447454f4ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2&lt;x&lt;Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/1/9f13c3435f98273ac95e512eb9aa55de88f781d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{xy}=a_xa_y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"12\" />, then (*) becomes:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/d/e2d3d6ceed307cc548161e6d7368876d8fc95a70.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Pa_P + Qa_Q - Qa_{2Q} = C_2\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>or\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/a/f9a7b5975004759c45327bc290f8a3d4dc7c1dfc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Pa_P - Qa_Q = C_2\\]\" width=\"135\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Case (iii): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/9/5398879cb35be74174b26b82a07ee2f632ae770d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3Q\\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" />. In this case, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/c/ffce335a846c1048a9d985eb07371ffc96eaf752.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_2=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/a/c7a7d7b05e3025229de54f94689726ec00842d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_3=-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"64\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/e/dfe0da219e49d7d770aad4046d6a16ccb30802fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> for all prime numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/7675de9415cd1198ebf2bc5e96bc56c1c8eccfb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3&lt;x&lt;Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"81\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/1/9f13c3435f98273ac95e512eb9aa55de88f781d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{xy}=a_xa_y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"12\" />, then (*) becomes:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/0/0f0f6f6d5599fd7e79f8989d0989c4cc1c5e152a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Pa_P + Qa_Q + 3Qa_{2Q} - 2Qa_{3Q} = C_3\\]\" width=\"281\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>or\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/7/d477934158aa8a84343aea46308bc5fad7596eda.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Pa_P + Qa_Q = C_3\\]\" width=\"135\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>In each case, by Bezout's Theorem, there exists non zero integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/8/b383531db14e9b5e0afdb30d93361dc804c65ccf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/2/4e288a80de4948fe6ed3adae0e8d9fc67d79fabb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> which satisfy the equation. For all other primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/3/c133cde61d496b9405d0342119842bf9728e6a10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p &gt; P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" />, just let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/7/eb72092f9a8e091d19849ae718d6c0a3ee09e4f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_p=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"16\" /> (or any other non-zero integer).\n</p><p>This construction is correct because, for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/8/5886e1fea88cfd56eb2d916d4c92eaf4f2bf064f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/0/080cf1d5376e10727a2af9488637a1d51f9c8459.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_k + 2 a_{2k} + \\cdots n a_{nk} =  a_k (1 + 2 a_2 + \\cdots n a_n ) = 0\\]\" width=\"379\" height=\"18\" /> \n</p><p>Since Bertrand's Theorem is not elementary, we still need to wait for a better proof.\n</p><p>--<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Lightest\" title=\"User:Lightest\">Lightest</a> 21:24, 2 May 2012 (EDT)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\">USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO\" title=\"2012 USAMO\">2012 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2012\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2012 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017012551\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.048 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.066 seconds\nPrep